    question: "What is a 401k?",
    answer: "A 401(k) is a tax-advantaged retirement savings plan offered by employers in the United States. Named after the section of the Internal Revenue Code that established it, a 401(k) allows employees to make pre-tax contributions directly from their paychecks.\n\nKey features of a 401(k) plan:\n\n1. Tax advantages: Contributions are made with pre-tax dollars, reducing your current taxable income. The money grows tax-deferred until withdrawal in retirement.\n\n2. Employer matching: Many employers match a portion of your contributions (e.g., 50% or 100% up to a certain percentage of your salary), which is essentially free money for your retirement.\n\n3. Contribution limits: For 2025, individuals can contribute up to $23,500 annually, with an additional $7,500 catch-up contribution allowed for those age 50 and older.\n\n4. Investment options: Typically offers a variety of investment options including mutual funds, target-date funds, and sometimes company stock.\n\n5. Withdrawal rules: Withdrawals before age 59½ generally incur a 10% early withdrawal penalty plus income taxes, with some exceptions for hardship.\n\n6. Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs): You must begin taking minimum distributions at age 73 (as of 2023 laws).\n\n7. Loan provisions: Some plans allow you to borrow from your 401(k), though this can impact your long-term growth.\n\nA 401(k) is one of the most effective ways to save for retirement, especially when taking full advantage of employer matching contributions.",
