    question: "What is a Roth IRA?",
    answer: "A Roth IRA is a special type of individual retirement account that offers tax-free growth and tax-free withdrawals in retirement. Unlike traditional IRAs, contributions to a Roth IRA are made with after-tax dollars, meaning you don't get a tax deduction when you contribute. However, the big advantage is that qualified withdrawals in retirement are completely tax-free.\n\nKey features of a Roth IRA:\n\n1. Tax Benefits: While contributions aren't tax-deductible, qualified withdrawals are 100% tax-free, including all earnings and investment gains.\n\n2. Contribution Limits: For 2025, individuals can contribute up to $7,000 annually ($8,000 if you're age 50 or older).\n\n3. Income Limits: There are income thresholds that may reduce or eliminate your ability to contribute to a Roth IRA. For 2025, the ability to contribute phases out between $146,000-$161,000 for single filers and $230,000-$240,000 for married couples filing jointly.\n\n4. No Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs): Unlike traditional IRAs, Roth IRAs have no required minimum distributions during the owner's lifetime, allowing the money to grow tax-free for as long as you want.\n\n5. Early Access to Contributions: You can withdraw your original contributions (but not earnings) at any time without penalties or taxes.\n\n6. Qualified Withdrawals: To withdraw earnings tax-free, the account must be open for at least 5 years, and you must be at least 59½ years old (with exceptions for first-time home purchases, certain educational expenses, or disability).\n\nRoth IRAs are particularly advantageous if you expect to be in a higher tax bracket in retirement than you are now, or if you want more tax flexibility in retirement. They're also an excellent legacy planning tool since heirs can also receive tax-free distributions.",
