    id: "i43",
    question: "What is an ETF?",
    answer: "An Exchange-Traded Fund (ETF) is an investment fund that trades on stock exchanges, similar to individual stocks. ETFs hold assets such as stocks, bonds, commodities, or a mix of these, and they typically aim to track the performance of a specific index, sector, commodity, or asset class.\n\nKey features of ETFs:\n\n1. Diversification: ETFs typically contain many different securities, providing instant diversification in a single purchase.\n\n2. Trading Flexibility: Unlike mutual funds which trade only once per day after market close, ETFs can be bought and sold throughout the trading day at market prices.\n\n3. Lower Costs: Most ETFs have lower expense ratios than comparable mutual funds, particularly passively managed index ETFs.\n\n4. Transparency: Most ETFs disclose their holdings daily, so investors always know what they own.\n\n5. Tax Efficiency: The structure of ETFs often results in fewer capital gains distributions compared to mutual funds, potentially reducing tax liability for investors.\n\n6. Variety: There are thousands of ETFs covering virtually every market, sector, and investment strategy imaginable, from broad market indexes to specific industries or investment themes.\n\nTypes of ETFs include:\n\n- Index ETFs: Track a specific index like the S&P 500 or NASDAQ\n- Sector ETFs: Focus on specific industries like technology, healthcare, or energy\n- Bond ETFs: Invest in various types of bonds (government, corporate, municipal)\n- International ETFs: Invest in foreign markets or regions\n- Commodity ETFs: Track the price of commodities like gold, oil, or agricultural products\n- Inverse ETFs: Designed to profit from a decline in the value of the underlying benchmark\n- Leveraged ETFs: Use financial derivatives to amplify returns (or losses)\n\nETFs have become increasingly popular for both new and experienced investors due to their simplicity, flexibility, and cost-effectiveness.",
