    id: "i44",
    question: "What is dollar-cost averaging?",
    answer: "Dollar-cost averaging (DCA) is an investment strategy where you invest a fixed amount of money at regular intervals, regardless of market conditions. Instead of investing a large sum all at once, you spread your investments over time.\n\nHow dollar-cost averaging works:\n\n1. You decide on a fixed amount to invest (e.g., $100, $500, etc.)\n2. You choose a regular schedule (weekly, monthly, quarterly)\n3. You automatically invest that amount regardless of whether the market is up or down\n\nKey benefits of dollar-cost averaging:\n\n1. Reduces the impact of market volatility: By investing regularly, you buy more shares when prices are low and fewer shares when prices are high, potentially lowering your average cost per share over time.\n\n2. Removes emotion from investing: The automatic nature of DCA helps prevent emotional decisions based on market fluctuations.\n\n3. Makes investing more accessible: Breaking investments into smaller, regular contributions makes investing more manageable for those who don't have large sums to invest at once.\n\n4. Creates good financial habits: Regular investing helps build discipline and consistency in your financial life.\n\nExample of dollar-cost averaging:\n\nImagine investing $100 monthly in a stock or fund with fluctuating prices:\n- Month 1: Price $20/share → You buy 5 shares\n- Month 2: Price $25/share → You buy 4 shares\n- Month 3: Price $16/share → You buy 6.25 shares\n\nAfter three months, you've invested $300 and own 15.25 shares, with an average cost of $19.67 per share, lower than the average price of $20.33 during this period.\n\nWhile dollar-cost averaging doesn't guarantee profits or protect against losses in declining markets, it's a prudent strategy for long-term investors, particularly those who are saving regularly from income and want to build wealth gradually.",
