    id: "i46",
    question: "What is compound interest?",
    answer: "Compound interest is often called the 'eighth wonder of the world' because of its powerful ability to grow wealth over time. It's the process where the interest you earn on an investment begins to generate its own interest, creating a snowball effect of growth.\n\nHow compound interest works:\n\nUnlike simple interest, which is calculated only on the initial principal, compound interest is calculated on both the principal amount and the accumulated interest from previous periods. This means your money grows at an accelerating rate over time.\n\nThe mathematical formula for compound interest is:\n\nA = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)\n\nWhere:\n- A = Final amount\n- P = Principal (initial investment)\n- r = Annual interest rate (decimal)\n- n = Number of times interest compounds per year\n- t = Time in years\n\nKey factors that affect compound interest:\n\n1. Interest Rate: Higher rates lead to faster growth.\n\n2. Time: The longer your money compounds, the more dramatic the growth becomes. This is why starting to save early is so important.\n\n3. Compounding Frequency: More frequent compounding (daily vs. monthly vs. annually) results in slightly higher returns.\n\n4. Regular Contributions: Adding money regularly supercharges the compounding effect.\n\nExample of compound interest:\n\nIf you invest $1,000 at 8% annual interest compounded yearly:\n- After 10 years: $2,159\n- After 20 years: $4,661\n- After 30 years: $10,063\n\nThis demonstrates the 'Rule of 72' - a quick way to estimate how long it takes for money to double. Divide 72 by the interest rate to get the approximate years needed (e.g., 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years to double).\n\nCompound interest works powerfully in your favor when saving and investing, but works against you with debt (especially credit cards and high-interest loans).",
