    answer: "Municipal bonds (or \"munis\") are debt securities issued by states, cities, counties, and other governmental entities to finance public projects such as schools, highways, hospitals, water systems, and other infrastructure needs.\n\nKey features of municipal bonds:\n\n1. Tax Advantages: The most distinctive feature of municipal bonds is that the interest income is generally exempt from federal income taxes. In many cases, if you buy bonds issued by your state of residence, the interest may also be exempt from state and local taxes (making them \"triple tax-exempt\").\n\n2. Credit Quality: Munis are typically categorized as either:\n   - General Obligation (GO) Bonds: Backed by the full faith, credit, and taxing power of the issuing government\n   - Revenue Bonds: Repaid from a specific revenue source (like toll road fees or utility payments)\n\n3. Maturity Options: Municipal bonds come in various maturities, from short-term (1-3 years) to long-term (20+ years).\n\n4. Income Generation: They typically provide regular, predictable interest payments (usually semi-annually).\n\n5. Lower Default Risk: Historically, municipal bonds have lower default rates than corporate bonds, though this varies by issuer.\n\n6. Liquidity: While they can be bought and sold in the secondary market, municipal bonds may be less liquid than some other investments.\n\nWho should consider municipal bonds?\n\n- Investors in higher tax brackets who can benefit most from the tax advantages\n- Investors seeking steady, predictable income\n- Conservative investors looking for investments with lower default risk\n- Investors wanting to diversify their portfolio beyond stocks and corporate bonds\n- Those looking to invest in their local communities\n\nPotential drawbacks:\n\n- Lower yields compared to taxable bonds (though the tax benefits may offset this)\n- Interest rate risk (bond values fall when interest rates rise)\n- Call risk (some bonds can be redeemed by the issuer before maturity)\n- May be subject to the Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT) in some cases\n\nMunicipal bonds are often purchased through a broker, bond fund, or ETF, with minimum investments typically starting at $5,000 for individual bonds.",
