    answer: "Checking and savings accounts serve different purposes in your financial life:\n\nChecking Account:\n- Designed for everyday transactions and frequent access\n- Typically comes with a debit card, checks, and online bill pay features\n- Usually has no or low interest rates\n- May have monthly maintenance fees (often waivable with direct deposit or minimum balance)\n- No withdrawal limits\n- Best for: Your everyday spending money and paying bills\n\nSavings Account:\n- Designed to hold money you're not planning to spend immediately\n- Earns interest (especially high-yield savings accounts)\n- Limited withdrawals (typically 6 per month under Federal Regulation D, though this has been temporarily lifted during the pandemic)\n- May require a minimum balance to avoid fees\n- Usually doesn't come with checks or debit cards\n- Best for: Emergency funds and saving for short-term goals\n\nMany financial experts recommend having both: a checking account for daily expenses and bill payments, and a savings account for your emergency fund and other savings goals. Some people even create multiple savings accounts for different goals (vacation, home down payment, etc.).",
