    id: "b4",
    question: "How do credit cards work?",
    answer: "Credit cards essentially provide a short-term loan that you can use for purchases and pay back later. Here's how they work:\n\n1. Credit Limit: When you're approved for a credit card, you're given a credit limit—the maximum amount you can spend on the card.\n\n2. Making Purchases: When you make a purchase, that amount is deducted from your available credit. You don't need to have any money in a bank account at the time of purchase.\n\n3. Grace Period: Credit cards typically offer a grace period of about 21-25 days between the end of a billing cycle and when your payment is due. If you pay your balance in full during this period, you won't be charged interest.\n\n4. Minimum Payments: Each month, you're required to make at least a minimum payment (usually 1-3% of your balance). However, only paying the minimum means you'll accrue interest on the remaining balance.\n\n5. Interest: If you don't pay your full balance by the due date, you'll be charged interest on the remaining amount. Credit card interest rates (APRs) are typically high, averaging around 16-24%.\n\n6. Credit Score Impact: Using a credit card responsibly (making on-time payments and keeping your balance low relative to your credit limit) can help build your credit score.\n\nThe key to using credit cards successfully is to treat them like a debit card—only spend what you can afford to pay off in full when the bill comes. This allows you to enjoy benefits like rewards points, consumer protections, and credit building without falling into costly debt.",
