    id: "b5",
    question: "What is compound interest and why is it important?",
    answer: "Compound interest is often called the 'eighth wonder of the world' because of its powerful effect on wealth building. It's the process of earning interest not just on your initial investment (the principal), but also on the interest you've already earned.\n\nHere's how compound interest works:\n\n1. You deposit money into an account that earns interest (like a savings account or investment)\n2. After the first period (day, month, or year), you earn interest on your initial deposit\n3. In the second period, you earn interest on both your initial deposit AND the interest you earned in the first period\n4. This cycle continues, with your money growing at an accelerating rate over time\n\nThe formula for compound interest is: A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt), where:\n- A is the final amount\n- P is the principal (initial investment)\n- r is the annual interest rate (as a decimal)\n- n is the number of times interest compounds per year\n- t is the time in years\n\nWhy is compound interest important? It demonstrates the time value of money and explains why starting to save early is so crucial. Even small amounts invested early in life can grow to substantial sums over decades.\n\nFor example, if you invest $1,000 at age 20 with an 8% annual return compounded yearly, it would grow to about $21,725 by age 65. But if you wait until age 35 to make that same $1,000 investment, it would only grow to about $10,063 by age 65.\n\nCompound interest works against you with debt, especially credit cards. High-interest debt compounds in the same way, which is why credit card balances can quickly spiral out of control if only minimum payments are made.",
