    answer: "A credit score is a three-digit number (typically ranging from 300 to 850) that represents your creditworthiness—or how likely you are to repay borrowed money. It's calculated based on information in your credit reports, which track your credit-related activities.\n\nThere are several credit scoring models, but the most common is the FICO Score. Here's what influences your FICO Score:\n\n- Payment history (35%): Whether you've paid bills on time\n- Credit utilization (30%): How much of your available credit you're using\n- Length of credit history (15%): How long you've had credit accounts\n- Credit mix (10%): The variety of credit accounts you have (cards, loans, etc.)\n- New credit (10%): How many new accounts you've recently opened\n\nScore ranges typically fall into these categories:\n- Excellent: 800-850\n- Very Good: 740-799\n- Good: 670-739\n- Fair: 580-669\n- Poor: 300-579\n\nWhy your credit score matters:\n\n1. Loan and credit card approvals: Higher scores increase your chances of approval\n2. Interest rates: Better scores mean lower interest rates on loans and credit cards\n3. Insurance premiums: In many states, insurers use credit-based insurance scores to set premiums\n4. Housing: Landlords often check credit scores when evaluating rental applications\n5. Employment: Some employers check credit reports (not scores) for certain positions\n6. Utility deposits: Good credit can help you avoid security deposits on utilities\n\nYou can check your credit score through many credit card issuers, banks, credit counseling services, or at AnnualCreditReport.com (which provides free access to your credit reports).",
