    question: "What's the difference between stocks, bonds, and mutual funds?",
    answer: "Stocks, bonds, and mutual funds are three fundamental investment types, each with distinct characteristics, risk profiles, and potential returns.\n\nStocks (Equities):\n- Represent ownership shares in a company\n- When you buy stock, you become a partial owner of that business\n- Potential returns come from: price appreciation and dividends\n- No guaranteed returns; values fluctuate based on company performance and market conditions\n- Historically provide higher long-term returns than bonds (about 10% annually before inflation)\n- Higher risk/volatility compared to bonds\n- Examples: Apple (AAPL), Amazon (AMZN), ExxonMobil (XOM)\n\nBonds (Fixed Income):\n- Essentially loans you make to an organization (government or corporation)\n- When you buy a bond, you become a creditor to the bond issuer\n- Potential returns come from: interest payments and return of principal at maturity\n- More predictable returns than stocks, but typically lower long-term performance\n- Historical returns around 5-6% annually before inflation\n- Generally lower risk than stocks but not risk-free (default risk, interest rate risk)\n- Examples: U.S. Treasury bonds, municipal bonds, corporate bonds\n\nMutual Funds:\n- Pooled investment vehicles managed by professionals\n- When you buy a mutual fund, you get exposure to many securities in one purchase\n- Types: Stock funds, bond funds, balanced funds (mix of stocks and bonds), specialty funds\n- Potential returns depend on underlying investments\n- Benefits: Diversification, professional management, relatively low minimum investments\n- Costs: Expense ratios (annual fees), possible load fees (sales charges)\n- Examples: Vanguard Total Stock Market Index Fund, Fidelity Contrafund\n\nRelated Investment Types:\n\nExchange-Traded Funds (ETFs):\n- Similar to mutual funds but trade like stocks throughout the day\n- Often have lower expense ratios than comparable mutual funds\n- Examples: SPDR S&P 500 ETF (SPY), Vanguard Total Bond Market ETF (BND)\n\nIndex Funds:\n- A type of mutual fund or ETF that tracks a specific market index\n- Passively managed (lower fees) compared to actively managed funds\n- Examples: Vanguard 500 Index Fund, Schwab U.S. Broad Market ETF\n\nWhen building a portfolio, most financial advisors recommend a mix of these investments based on your time horizon, risk tolerance, and financial goals. Younger investors typically allocate more to stocks for growth, while investors near retirement often shift toward bonds for stability and income.",
