    question: "How much does it cost to set up and maintain a trust?",
    answer: "Trust costs vary significantly based on complexity, assets, and location. Understanding these costs helps you determine if a trust makes financial sense for your situation.\n\nSetup Costs:\n\n1. Simple Revocable Trust:\n• DIY software: $50-$200\n• Basic attorney: $1,000-$3,000\n• Comprehensive attorney: $3,000-$5,000\n\n2. Complex Irrevocable Trust:\n• Attorney fees: $3,000-$10,000+\n• Specialized trusts (charitable, dynasty): $5,000-$25,000+\n\n3. Additional Setup Expenses:\n• Asset transfer costs (retitling accounts, deeds): $500-$2,000\n• Appraisal fees for real estate or business: $500-$5,000\n• Tax preparation for trust tax returns: $500-$2,000 annually\n\nOngoing Maintenance Costs:\n\n1. Professional Trustee Fees:\n• Corporate trustees: 0.5%-1.5% of assets annually\n• Individual trustees: $0-$50,000+ annually (varies widely)\n• Minimum fees: Often $2,500-$10,000 per year regardless of asset size\n\n2. Administrative Expenses:\n• Tax preparation: $500-$3,000 annually\n• Investment management: 0.25%-1.5% of assets annually\n• Legal updates/modifications: $500-$5,000 as needed\n• Accounting and record keeping: $1,000-$5,000 annually\n\nCost-Benefit Analysis:\n\n• Trusts generally make sense for estates over $100,000-$500,000\n• Potential savings from avoiding probate, taxes, or creditor claims often exceed costs\n• Consider the value of professional management and peace of mind\n\nWays to Minimize Costs:\n\n• Start with simpler trust structures\n• Serve as your own trustee initially (revocable trusts)\n• Use fee-only financial advisors rather than commission-based\n• Bundle multiple services with one provider\n• Review and compare trustee fees regularly\n\nThe key is ensuring the benefits (tax savings, asset protection, peace of mind) outweigh the costs.",
